The two halves of working with raw storage: object lifetime, and byte primitives. Containers
of layer 5 are built from these; user code rarely needs them except to write a container.

### Object lifetime

| Function | Does |
|---|---|
| `construct_at(p, args...)` | constructs a T at p (placement new, without `<new>`) |
| `destroy_at(p)`, `destroy_n(p, n)` | ends the lifetime of one or n objects, in order |
| `copy_construct_n(from, n, to)` | copy-constructs n objects into uninitialized storage |
| `relocate_n(from, n, to)` | moves n objects to uninitialized storage and ends the sources |
| `relocate_overlapping_n(from, n, to)` | the same within one array, ranges overlapping |
| `launder(p)` | a pointer to the object now living where p points |

Relocation is the operation a growing array needs: move-construct each element at its new
place, then destroy the old one. For a trivially relocatable type the pair is a single
memcpy. `ca::is_trivially_relocatable<T>` holds for trivially copyable types and for types that
opt in with `static constexpr bool TRIVIALLY_RELOCATABLE = true`; a type may opt in when
nothing points into it and nothing registers its address, which is true of most owning types
(strings, arrays, unique pointers).

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"

struct buffer final {
	static constexpr bool TRIVIALLY_RELOCATABLE = true;
	int* data;
	ca::usz size;
	explicit buffer(const ca::usz n) noexcept : data{nullptr}, size{n} {
	}
	buffer(buffer&& other) noexcept : data{other.data}, size{other.size} {
		other.data = nullptr;
	}
	~buffer(void) noexcept {
	}
};

static_assert(ca::is_trivially_relocatable<buffer>);
static_assert(ca::is_trivially_relocatable<int>);

auto main(void) -> int {
	alignas(buffer) ca::byte from[2U * sizeof(buffer)];
	alignas(buffer) ca::byte to[2U * sizeof(buffer)];
	buffer* const src = reinterpret_cast<buffer*>(from);
	buffer* const dst = reinterpret_cast<buffer*>(to);
	ca::construct_at(src, 3U);
	ca::construct_at(src + 1, 5U);
	ca::relocate_n(src, 2U, dst);
	const bool moved = dst[0].size == 3U && dst[1].size == 5U;
	ca::destroy_n(dst, 2U);
	return moved ? 0 : 1;
}
```

`placement_new.hpp` declares the only placement `operator new` ca uses, tagged with a private
type so it never collides with the one of `<new>` when both are visible; call `construct_at`
rather than writing `new` directly.

### Bytes and code units

| Function | C equivalent |
|---|---|
| `copy_bytes(to, from, n)` | memcpy |
| `move_bytes(to, from, n)` | memmove |
| `fill_bytes(to, value, n)` | memset |
| `compare_bytes(a, b, n)` | memcmp, constexpr |
| `find_byte(p, c, n)` | memchr |
| `string_length(s)` | strlen, any code unit type, constexpr |
| `compare_units`, `equal_units`, `find_unit` | memcmp, memchr over char16_t and char32_t too |

Each takes a count of 0 with any pointer, null included, unlike the C functions. `copy_bytes`,
`move_bytes`, `fill_bytes`, `compare_bytes`, `find_byte` and `string_length` are the
compiler's builtins, so small constant ones become a few moves or fold away; the calls left
over reach the memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, bcmp, memchr and strlen that ca itself defines
on x86-64 (see `memory/impl/`), so no libc is needed for them. Built integer-only
(`-mgeneral-regs-only` or `-mno-sse`, where `ca_target_feature(sse2)` is 0), those seven work
on 64-bit words and `rep movsb`/`rep stosb` and touch no vector register, for code that runs
where the vector state is not saved; the SSE2 and AVX2 variants and the out-of-line
`ca::move_paths` are left out of such a build.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"

static_assert(ca::string_length("hello") == 5U);
static_assert(ca::string_length(u"wide") == 4U);
static_assert(ca::compare_bytes("abc", "abd", 3U) < 0);

auto main(void) -> int {
	char line[16] = "key=value";
	const char* const eq = ca::find_byte(line, '=', ca::string_length(line));
	if (eq == nullptr || eq - line != 3)
		return 1;
	ca::move_bytes(line + 1, line, 3U);
	ca::fill_bytes(line, ca::u8{'_'}, 1U);
	char copy[16];
	ca::copy_bytes(copy, line, sizeof(line));
	return ca::compare_bytes(copy, "_keyvalue", 10U) == 0 ? 0 : 2;
}
```

`max_alignment` is the alignment `malloc` and `operator new` guarantee, `alignof(max_align_t)`,
taken from the compiler and checked against `<stddef.h>` when the library builds.
